            0.126,
            0.1,
            0.822,
            0.12
          ],
          "content": "**4. Explain the nature of the covalent bond using the bond formation in** $\\mathrm{CH_{3}Cl}$ **.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.121,
            0.481,
            0.138
          ],
          "content": "**5. Draw the electron dot structures for**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.141,
            0.334,
            0.158
          ],
          "content": "**(a) ethanoic acid.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.161,
            0.251,
            0.179
          ],
          "content": "**(b)** $\\mathrm { H _ { 2 } S . }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.181,
            0.314,
            0.198
          ],
          "content": "**(c) propanone.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.2,
            0.237,
            0.219
          ],
          "content": "**(d)** $\\mathrm { F _ { 2 } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.22,
            0.664,
            0.238
          ],
          "content": "**6. What is an homologous series? Explain with an example.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.24,
            0.87,
            0.273
          ],
          "content": "**7. How can ethanol and ethanoic acid be differentiated on the basis of their physical and chemical properties?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.276,
            0.87,
            0.309
          ],
          "content": "**8. Why does micelle formation take place when soap is added to water? Will a micelle be formed in other solvents such as ethanol also?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.312,
            0.8,
            0.33
          ],
          "content": "**9. Why are carbon and its compounds used as fuels for most applications?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.332,
            0.765,
            0.35
          ],
          "content": "**10. Explain the formation of scum when hard water is treated with soap.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.351,
            0.862,
            0.369
          ],
          "content": "**11. What change will you observe if you test soap with litmus paper (red and blue)?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.372,
            0.678,
            0.389
          ],
          "content": "**12. What is hydrogenation? What is its industrial application?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.391,
            0.733,
            0.409
          ],
          "content": "**13. Which of the following hydrocarbons undergo addition reactions:**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.407,
            0.441,
            0.427
          ],
          "content": "$\\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6},C_{3}H_{8},C_{3}H_{6},C_{2}H_{2}}$ and $\\mathrm { C H } _ { 4 }$ ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.427,
            0.869,
            0.461
          ],
          "content": "**14. Give a test that can be used to differentiate between saturated and unsaturated hydrocarbons.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.463,
            0.644,
            0.482
          ],
          "content": "**15. Explain the mechanism of the cleaning action of soaps.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.517,
            0.432,
            0.546
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Group Activity"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.563,
            0.863,
            0.596
          ],
          "content": "I **Use molecular model kits to make models of the compounds you have learnt in this Chapter.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.599,
            0.865,
            0.665
          ],
          "content": "II **Take about 20 mL of castor oil/cotton seed oil/linseed oil/soyabean oil in a beaker. Add 30 mL of 20 % sodium hydroxide solution. Heat the mixture with continuous stirring for a few minutes till the mixture thickens. Add 5-10 g of common salt to this. Stir the mixture well and allow it to cool.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.667,
            0.863,
            0.701
          ],
          "content": "**You can cut out the soap in fancy shapes. You can also add perfume to the soap before it sets.**"
        },
